<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tolpuddle Martyrs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tolpuddle Martyrs were six agricultural labourers from Tolpuddle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, convicted in 1834 of swearing a secret oath as members of a friendly society. Led by the Methodist local preacher George Loveless, they had formed the Friendly Society of Agricultural Labourers in 1833 to protest against wage cuts. Charged under the Unlawful Oaths Act of 1797, they were sentenced to seven years' transportation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. After mass protests and the collection of 800,000 signatures for their release, they were pardoned in March 1836 and returned to England between 1837 and 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tolpuddle-martyrs.jpg" alt="Tolpuddle Martyrs" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Bishop · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tolpuddle_Martyrs_plaque_London_Ontario.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
